discussion_timeline.body:
@Nik-09 The changes in PR LGTM, I'll approve the PR once the two threads are resolved:

1. https://github.com/oppia/oppia/pull/13065#discussion_r657315759
2. https://github.com/oppia/oppia/pull/13065#discussion_r655730531

> @seanlip Can we retrieve a ClassifierTrainingJob by its non-key attributes (exp_id, exp_version, state_name)? 

@pranavsid98 I don't understand the question. Could you please rephrase/elaborate?

One common approach, if keys are deterministically constructed, is to reconstruct the key and then do a get_by_id().

@prasanna08 I like the way this check happens. You're suggesting that we just create a new job when we encounter this obsolete case and invalidate the older job at the time of fetching? That sounds neat. So, in every job that we fetch, we check if the exploration's last version corresponds to the job's version. Thats brilliant actually, I wonder why we didn't think of this before.

@seanlip Btw, I've added the create_multi for a single PUT operation. So, as Prasanna said, other than a few minor infrastructural changes, the above case is the only blocker left.

EDIT: My bad, there is one more case left which I'll add soon. There is the case when the only change is the statee_name and there is no change in answer group. This case is left as well.

@seanlip .
Oh yes, that is true. Adding a field will be like adding bits and pieces.
If the mapping was to a job instead of a classifier, we could always retrieve the classifier. The job doesn't even need an additional classifier_id field. The classifier is instantiated with the job_id. So, we can use the job's id to retrieve its corresponding classifier. This is actually a great solution. Sorry, it took me some time to figure that out.

@anmolshkl @prasanna08 What do you think?

@prasanna08 . I was just adding support for the case where we encounter renamed states and it works perfectly. I've got a small concern though. The function get_trainable_states_dict() is called from within _save_exploration(). Now, with the recent addition, this function also accesses commit logs to check for states that are renamed. So, to access commit logs, commit logs need to be present. This makes it such that we can't test this function exclusively. Let's say I call update_exploration() or save_Explortion() from within this test to make a commit log, this update_exploration() will in turn trigger get_trainable_states_dict anyway. This way of testing is the exact same way we tested in classifier_services.py. So, the tests sort of become redundant since both tests do the same thing. The tests from classifier_services are executing (I added one more test to check if renaming a state without changing answer group triggers job creation - it doesnt). 
@seanlip Can we skip tests for the above function? That function is tested anyway in classifier_services indirectly.

Oh, and Sean, I actually think we need a separate classifier_id if we need to avoid a second GET operation. job_id will always be present, but the classifier corresponding to that job_id need not be stored yet. So, if we have just the job_id, we can't really say if a classifier is present without doing a GET.

> The only thing left to do is to add tests :)

@kevinlee12 Should I implement a test for this script? I don't have any experience with test in Python actually.

> I think that sounds correct. Just this script would need to be run. Also, @kevinlee12, on circleCI, would we need both the frontend tests and this coverage check? It would be a redundant run of the frontend tests right? Also, one question here @marianazangrossi what would happen if the frontend tests fail? Would your script exit at that point?

@nithusha21 I've changed the script so if frontend tests fails, the check will not happen.
